From bffbd105deee809c49cd2be7446b5aae815e513e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 16:15:05 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 116/126] decode-link: what the stand against real servers and
 two reviews showed

The stand fetches a file through every outbound made of a link: servers
of Xray, Hysteria and sing-box with an inbound per protocol, transport
and security, and links to them written by the generators of Marzban and
Remnawave and by the Hysteria program. Four kinds of link gave an
outbound that was built and carried nothing:

- WebSocket, HTTPUpgrade, gRPC and HTTP/2 with an ALPN in the link.
  Panels write one ALPN for every host ("h2,http/1.1"). Xray asks for
  http/1.1 with the first two and for h2 with the others whatever the
  link says; sing-box asks for what is set, and a server offered both
  picks HTTP/2, over which no WebSocket handshake goes. The ALPN is left
  to such a transport; sb.tls.alpn still sets it.
- The QUIC transport: the outbound had uTLS (the fingerprint a link has
  by default), and every connection failed with "unsupported usage for
  uTLS". With no ALPN in the link sing-box offered h3 alone, which a
  server of Xray does not take: the outbound offers h3, h2 and http/1.1.
- A gRPC service name that is a custom path of several segments, as
  Marzban writes it (escaped once more than the link, "/Tun" left out):
  refused like the same path written plainly.
- HTTP/2 without TLS: sing-box speaks HTTP/1.1 there and Xray h2c. A
  warning: a server of sing-box takes both.

A link is foreign text, and what it may do is bounded:

- A value that is a JSON object is refused for a path of an option: the
  names inside it would pass by the list of what a link may not set.
- tls.handshake_timeout and tls.fragment_fallback_delay join that list:
  with them a link held every connection of its outbound for as long as
  it said. The name Throne has for the second is no longer read.
- The option cert of the Shadowsocks plugin v2ray-plugin names a local
  file, which sing-box reads when it builds the outbound: refused, as
  the options that end in _path are.
- A list of ports of Hysteria that names more ports than there are is
  refused: sing-box writes the ranges out port by port, and a list that
  repeats a range took the memory of the router. A range is read as
  sing-box reads it, open ends included.
- tls.spoof with tcp_fast_open is refused: every connection of such an
  outbound fails.
- The building of the outbound is bounded to 20 s and a failure of its
  code (a panic in a constructor) is one line of error: a link that
  cannot be used, not a crash of the command.

The messages do not repeat the link:

- A parameter whose name is not a name (letters, digits, "-", "_", ".",
  128 at most) is left out with one warning: what stands in the place of
  a name may be anything, another link with its user included. The
  "amp;" of a link copied out of HTML is dropped from the name as it is
  shown and as it is looked up.
- Where sing-box would quote a part of the link that is not a name, the
  decoder answers first: a TUIC user that is not a UUID, a Shadowsocks
  method or an obfuscation of Hysteria 2 that is not a name, a REALITY
  short id that is not up to sixteen hexadecimal digits.
- What sing-box says of an outbound it refuses is printed without the
  values of the outbound that are not plain names, as without its
  secrets. A flow and the type of a transport set by their paths, and
  the keys and values of the JSON in the parameter extra, are not
  repeated as they are. Every error and every warning is one line.

As the client of Xray reads it:

- The security of a VMess link other than aes-128-gcm and
  chacha20-poly1305 is auto, with a warning: its server refuses none,
  zero and aes-128-cfb.

A TUIC link without an ALPN gets a warning (a server that has one set
refuses such a client), and a path that sets a list of one to what the
link has set already says nothing.
